{
 "name": "reflectron TOF (orthogonal acceleration)",
 "notes": "Domain trimmed to the active region: x sliced to [55, 95] mm of the original frame and re-zeroed (x' = x - 55); 0-55 mm was field-free vacuum. All x positions in the notes below are the OLD frame. Source plates are clipped at the new left edge; certification below proves the beam-region field is unchanged. Orthogonal-acceleration reflectron TOF, after the oaR-TOFMS of Peng et al. 2021 (Chin. J. Anal. Chem. 49(3) e21029). Chain, in flight order: a continuous beam crosses the ORTHOGONAL ACCELERATOR left-to-right at y = 10 mm with 2 eV of beam energy; the pusher (1180 V) and two extraction GRIDS (1000 V, 0 V) accelerate the packet upward in a two-field Wiley-McLaren source; it coasts through a grounded FLIGHT TUBE; a gridded linear ion mirror (26-step ladder to V_back 1150 V) reflects and energy-focuses it; the retained beam energy carries it sideways so it lands on the DETECTOR plate beside the source. Every flight starts in the accelerator and ends on metal -- nothing begins or ends in free space. Native ion_gym build (no external geometry import). RESOLUTION IS NOT CONVERGED -- DO NOT QUOTE IT (found 2026-08-10). Halving the cell size (0.5 -> 0.25 mm) changes R_fwhm for m/z 500 from 372 to 65, and re-tuning V_back at the fine grid gives an ERRATIC response (R_fwhm 65 / 19 / 10 / 32 / 10 at 1150/1170/1190/1210/1230 V) instead of the smooth optimum a reflectron must have. The sharp optimum seen at 0.5 mm was therefore substantially a discretization artifact: the mirror voltage was compensating a grid-dependent field error, not a designed focus. Transmission (48/48) and the flight-time scale ARE robust to the refinement (TOF moves < 0.6%); only the packet width is unconverged. This example is sound for teaching the CHAIN and the sensitivity of a mirror tune; it is not a performance benchmark. To approach real instrument figures (Peng 2021 reports 6500 FWHM at m/z 502) the design needs, in order: a converged cell size for the mirror ladder, an analytically designed Wiley-McLaren space focus AT the detector plane (rather than compensating with V_back), a TWO-STAGE mirror for second-order energy focusing (this one is single-stage = first-order only), and a longer flight path (0.34 m here). Numbers below are AT 0.5 mm CELLS (2 eV beam, packet 3.0 x 1.6 mm born at x = 65, y = 10; vacuum; m/z 100 and 500, 24 ions each): TRANSMISSION 48/48 to the detector; m/z 100 arrives at 10.32 us with a 22.4 ns packet spread (R = t/2dt = 230), m/z 500 at 23.07 us with 46.6 ns (R = 248); landing spot x = 83.8-86.9 mm on a detector spanning 76-90 mm, clear of the outgoing leg at x ~ 65 mm. The time focus is SHARP in the mirror voltage: +-10 V on V_back costs roughly half the resolution (R 230 -> 133 at 1160 V, -> 36 at 1140 V), which makes this a good example for showing students what a reflectron tune actually buys. DESIGN NOTE (why grids): the extraction plates and mirror ladder are declared is_grid, so they are Dirichlet boundaries in the solve but transparent to ions -- the textbook Wiley-McLaren + reflectron instrument. An earlier slit/aperture (gridless) version of this same layout transmitted only ~7/48: an exit aperture is a diverging lens (f ~ 4V/dE ~ 52 mm here) and aperture leakage peaks on its own axis, so any off-axis offset grows. Gridless planar mirrors are the advanced case and need dedicated compensation; see the SLIM/transaxial work rather than this example. Geometry generator: tools/make_oatof_example.py.",
